          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"draw-a-shape-project-it-look-at-it\">Draw a shape, project it, look at it</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Paint a shape in the canvas at the top. Every point becomes a row of a\nmatrix <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mathbf{X}||)</marimo-tex> with two columns, its <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(y||)</marimo-tex> position. Multiplying\nby the <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(2 \\times 4||)</marimo-tex> matrix <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mathbf{W}||)</marimo-tex> turns that into an <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(n \\times 4||)</marimo-tex>\nmatrix <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mathbf{Z} = \\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}||)</marimo-tex>. Adding gaussian noise gives\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\tilde{\\mathbf{Z}} = \\mathbf{Z} + \\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}||)</marimo-tex>, and the pair\nplot at the bottom shows how its four columns are distributed.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The default <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mathbf{W}||)</marimo-tex> keeps <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(y||)</marimo-tex> and adds <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x + y||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x - y||)</marimo-tex>.\nDrag its numbers to mix the columns differently, and the slider to set\nthe standard deviation of the noise (in canvas pixels).</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
